schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Provenance (vs. lineage)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    The recorded origin and history of a piece of knowledge — where it came
    from, who made it, and how it changed — distinguished from "lineage,"
    which is the path data took through systems. Provenance answers "why do
    we believe this, and who said it first?"
  source_traditions:
    - data-engineering (data lineage vs. provenance distinction)
    - archival/records science (chain of custody)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Directly maps to this framework's own `provenance` mixin
    (schemas/provenance.yaml: origin, surfaced_by, adapted_from,
    transformation, authorship) — the mechanism that keeps "inclusion does
    not mean endorsement" honest by making origin traceable on every object.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "data lineage (the technical path through systems — a related but distinct concept per the Snowflake source cited)"
  important_distinctions:
    - "lineage = the path data took through systems; provenance = the historical record of origin, custody, and trust"
  tensions:
    - "provenance is the highest-leverage habit for a long-lived commons, but it is also the part contributors most often skip because it feels like overhead in the moment"
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Losing provenance lets claims decay into folklore — repeated by everyone,
    backed by no one — which is exactly the failure mode a knowledge commons
    is trying to avoid by recording origin every time.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/source-systems.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
